==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)kep-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|shap}}, {{m|enm|schape}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġesceap||shape, form, created being, creature, creation, dispensation, fate, condition, sex, gender, genitalia}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*ga-}} + {{m|gmw-pro|*skap}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*ga-}} + {{m|gem-pro|*skapą||shape, nature, condition}}, possibly from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)kep-||to split, cut}}. 

The verb is from {{inh|en|enm|shapen}}, {{m|enm|schapen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|scieppan||to shape, form, make, create, assign, arrange, destine, order, adjudge}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*skappjan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*skapjaną||to create}}, from the noun. 

The noun is cognate with {{cog|dum|schap||form}}, {{cog|gmh|geschaf||creature}}, {{cog|is|skap||state, condition, temper, mood}}. The verb is cognate with {{cog|nl|scheppen}}, {{cog|de|schaffen}}, {{cog|sv|skapa||create, make}}, {{cog|nb|skape||create}}. {{doublet|en|-ship}}.

